---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Shot from "../../components/Shot.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Business Operations System Concept Demo"
  description="A working concept demonstration of an SME operations system: customers, inventory, orders, tasks, reporting and settings. Built by Forthmark Systems with fictional data."
  image="/images/work/ops-preview.png"
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="chip chip--concept">Concept demonstration</span>
      <div class="page-head__grid mt-2">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Concept demo / Business operations</p>
          <h1 class="h1">Business operations system</h1>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">
          A working demo of the operational core many growing businesses need:
          customer records, stock, orders, tasks and reporting in one place. Every
          record is fictional.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="fact-row">
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Type</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Concept demonstration</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Data</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Fictional, stored locally</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Scope</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Customers, stock, orders, tasks, reports</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/demos/operations/">
          Open the working demo <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact">Discuss similar work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="callout callout--concept">
        <p>
          <strong>This is not a client deployment.</strong> Northline Supply Co. is
          an invented business. The customers, products, orders, tasks and figures
          are fictional and exist only to demonstrate how the system behaves. No
          real company data is used.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-3">
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/ops-preview.png"
          alt="Operations demo dashboard showing revenue, open orders, low stock, tasks due, a six month revenue chart and recent orders"
          caption="Dashboard: sales, open orders, stock attention and tasks due, calculated from the same fictional records."
          tag="Concept demo"
          wide
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="sticky-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
        <h2 class="h2">One place for the working truth.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A growing business often runs on a spreadsheet for stock, a messaging
          group for orders, an inbox for enquiries and one person's memory for
          follow up. Each tool works in isolation. Together they create daily
          reconciliation work and hide problems until they are expensive.
        </p>
        <p>
          This demo shows a different arrangement. A customer is recorded once. A
          product has one stock figure. An order references both and changes status
          as it moves. Tasks carry owners and due dates. The dashboard and reports
          read from the same records rather than asking someone to rebuild the
          numbers each month.
        </p>
        <p>
          The interaction is real. You can search, filter, edit, change statuses,
          adjust stock, add records, export CSV files and reset the workspace. State
          is held in your browser so the demo survives a page refresh without a
          server.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper2">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="section-head__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">What it covers</p>
          <h2 class="h2">The unglamorous core.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            The demo is deliberately ordinary. These are the records most small
            businesses actually need before anything advanced.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Customers</h3>
          <p>
            Contact records with company, segment and history. Search and filter,
            then open a customer to see the orders attached to them.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Inventory</h3>
          <p>
            Products with SKU, category, price, stock and reorder level. Stock can
            be adjusted in one click, and low or out of stock items are flagged.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Orders</h3>
          <p>
            Orders link to a customer and several products. Status moves through
            pending, processing, fulfilled or cancelled, and totals are calculated
            from the line items.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Tasks</h3>
          <p>
            Work with an owner, priority and due date. Overdue items rise to the
            top so the team can see what needs attention without a meeting.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Reports</h3>
          <p>
            Revenue, order counts, stock exposure and task completion, generated
            from the same records. A summary can be exported as CSV.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Settings</h3>
          <p>
            Business name, operations email and currency. Change them and the
            workspace updates. A reset returns the demo to its seeded state.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="gallery mt-3">
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/ops-inventory.png"
          alt="Inventory view of the operations demo with product search, category filter, stock counts, reorder levels and stock adjustment buttons"
          caption="Inventory: stock levels, reorder points and direct adjustment controls."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/ops-orders.png"
          alt="Orders table in the operations demo showing order numbers, fictional customers, dates, totals, status selectors and view buttons"
          caption="Orders: status changes happen in the list, with line item detail available per order."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/ops-reports.png"
          alt="Reports view with revenue by month, order status counts, top products and stock exposure"
          caption="Reports: the same records summarised without a separate spreadsheet."
          tag="Concept demo"
          wide
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Interactions</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What actually works.</h2>
        <p class="lede mt-1">
          The demo is not a clickable image. These actions change the stored state
          and are reflected across the views.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Search and filter customers, products and orders.</li>
          <li>Add or edit a customer, product or task.</li>
          <li>Change an order status and see the dashboard totals respond.</li>
          <li>Increase or reduce stock, with low stock warnings recalculated.</li>
          <li>Mark a task complete and update the due task count.</li>
          <li>Export orders or a report summary as a CSV file.</li>
          <li>Reset the demo to the original fictional dataset.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout mt-2">
          <p>
            <strong>Data handling:</strong> records are stored in this browser
            using localStorage. Nothing is sent to a server, and clearing browser
            storage or resetting the demo removes your changes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper2">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What this demo is not.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A concept demo should be honest about its limits. This one is a front end
          prototype with local state. It does not include the parts a production
          deployment would require.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>No login, roles or permissions. Anyone with the page can change the demo data.</li>
          <li>No server or shared database. Two browsers do not see the same records.</li>
          <li>No payment processing, invoicing or accounting integration.</li>
          <li>No email, SMS or WhatsApp notifications.</li>
          <li>No stock reservation during checkout, because there is no checkout.</li>
          <li>No audit trail or record recovery beyond resetting the demo.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">
          These are scope decisions, not hidden gaps. A real engagement would agree
          which of them matter, then build and test them properly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">From demo to deployment</p>
        <h2 class="h2">How this maps to real work.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A production version starts with the same questions the demo answers:
          what is a customer, what is a product, how does an order move, who owns a
          task, and which numbers does management actually read?
        </p>
        <p>
          From there, a real build adds authentication, a shared database,
          role based access, validation, backups, email notifications and the
          specific rules of the business. The interface patterns demonstrated here
          remain useful, but they are the starting point rather than the finish.
        </p>
        <div class="callout mt-2">
          <p>
            <strong>Technology note.</strong> The demo is built with Astro and
            vanilla JavaScript, with no framework runtime. CSV exports are generated
            in the browser and state is stored locally.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="btn-row mt-3">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/demos/operations/">
            Try the demo <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/work/programme-platform">
            See the programme demo
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Need a system that matches how you work?"
    text="Bring the spreadsheet, the process and the exceptions. We will help you decide what the first useful release should contain."
    primaryLabel="Discuss a project"
    secondaryLabel="All work"
    secondaryHref="/work"
  />
</BaseLayout>
